        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          The deck does not know what month it is. It knows how much water is in the boards. Measure that, and the question of when to stain answers itself.
        </p>

        <h2 id="why-wait" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. Why Waiting Matters at All
        </h2>

        <p>
          A stain, whether it is a penetrating oil or a water-based semi-transparent, works by moving into the wood. The resin and pigment travel down into the top fibres, carried by solvent or water, and cure there. For that to happen the cell structure of the board has to have room. Wood that is still full of water has no room. The stain sits on the surface, cures as a thin skin, and either peels off within a season or dries blotchy where some boards happened to be drier than others.
        </p>

        <p>
          Pressure-treated lumber is the worst case because of how it is made. Southern yellow pine is loaded into a cylinder, the air is pulled out, and a water-borne preservative — ACQ, copper azole or micronised copper — is forced into the wood under pressure. The boards come out of that process at 35 to 60 percent moisture content, and sometimes higher. That is not damp. That is wet through, and it is why fresh PT lumber is heavy, cold to the touch and often visibly beaded with moisture when you buy it. Stores do not dry it. It goes from the treatment plant to the rack to your truck in that state.
        </p>

        <p>
          The number stain manufacturers want is somewhere below 15 percent moisture content. Some labels say 12, some allow up to 19, but 15 is the working target for most of the products we use. Getting a two-inch-thick treated board from 40 percent to 15 percent takes weeks of warm, dry air moving around it, and it takes considerably longer when the boards are stacked, shaded, or sitting in a humid climate.
        </p>

        <p>
          Cedar and redwood have a different problem. They are usually kiln-dried at the mill to 15 to 19 percent, so they arrive close to stainable in terms of moisture. What they carry instead is <strong>mill glaze</strong>: a hard, burnished surface left by the planer knives, made worse by the natural extractives these species bring to the surface when the wood is heated by the cutter. Water beads on mill glaze exactly as it beads on wet wood, and stain sits on it exactly the same way. The wait for cedar is not about drying. It is about weathering or cleaning that glaze off.
        </p>

        <p>
          So "how long to wait" is really two separate questions. For pressure-treated wood, the question is how long until the moisture is out. For cedar and redwood, the question is how long until the surface will accept a finish. The good news is that both questions have the same test, and we will get to it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="timelines" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. The Real Timelines by Material
        </h2>

        <p>
          Every timeline below is a range because climate dominates everything. A deck in Phoenix in June dries in a fraction of the time a deck in Seattle in March does. Treat these as a guide for when to start testing, not as a permission slip to stain on a given day.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">KDAT Pressure-Treated: One to Two Weeks</h3>
        <p>
          Kiln-dried-after-treatment lumber goes back into the kiln after the preservative has been driven in, and it leaves the yard at roughly 19 percent or below. It is lighter, straighter, and it costs more — usually a 20 to 40 percent premium, and you often have to order it. If your deck was built from KDAT, you can clean and brighten it as soon as construction is finished, let it dry for a couple of days, and stain it within the first week or two. This is the one scenario where "stain a new deck right away" is genuinely correct, and it is one of the strongest arguments for paying the premium in the first place.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Standard Wet Pressure-Treated: Four Weeks to Three Months or More</h3>
        <p>
          This is the deck most people have. In a hot, dry climate with the deck in full sun — Texas, Arizona, inland California in summer — the boards can be ready in four to six weeks. In the Mid-Atlantic, the Midwest or the Southeast in a normal summer, plan on eight to twelve weeks. In the Pacific Northwest, New England in a wet year, or on a deck that spends most of the day in shade, three months is common and four is not unusual. Decks finished in autumn in a cold climate will usually not be ready until the following late spring, because the wood does not dry meaningfully through a cold, damp winter.
        </p>

        <p>
          One complication: some treatment plants add a wax-based water repellent to the preservative, and the lumber is sold as "water repellent" or with a brand name suggesting it is pre-sealed. This wax slows the drying and slows stain penetration for months. The sprinkle test catches it, but it is worth knowing that this lumber sits at the long end of every range above.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cedar and Redwood: Two to Eight Weeks</h3>
        <p>
          Kiln-dried cedar and redwood are close to the right moisture content on delivery, so the wait is about the surface. Left alone, mill glaze weathers off in six to eight weeks of sun and rain. Cleaned and brightened, it comes off in an afternoon, and the deck can be stained two or three days later once the wood has dried from the wash. Two weeks is a sensible minimum to let the construction moisture and any rain-soaking equalise; eight weeks is the point after which you are starting to lose surface to UV. Green (air-dried) cedar from a small mill is a different matter and behaves more like wet PT — test it. Our product picks for the species are in the <a href="/best-deck-stain-for-cedar/">best deck stain for cedar</a> guide.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Tropical Hardwoods: Days, Not Months</h3>
        <p>
          Ipe, cumaru, tigerwood and the like are kiln-dried, dense and oily. They do not have a drying problem; they have a penetration problem, because the wood is so tight that most stains barely get in. The usual approach is a light clean, a wipe with the manufacturer's recommended solvent to remove surface oils, and a thin coat of a hardwood-specific penetrating oil such as Penofin Hardwood Formula or Ipe Oil within the first couple of weeks. Waiting longer just means the surface silvers and you have to clean it back first.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Composite and PVC: Never</h3>
        <p>
          A note because the question comes up constantly. Capped composite decking — current-generation Trex, TimberTech AZEK, Fiberon and the rest — has a plastic shell bonded over a wood-plastic core, and PVC decking is plastic all the way through. Stain has nothing to soak into and nothing to bond to. It does not get stained, ever, and the manufacturer's warranty will say so. Older uncapped composite from the early 2000s can fade and go blotchy, and there are composite-specific coatings for it, but the honest answer for most of those decks is a good clean rather than a coating. If you have composite, your "when to stain" question is answered: you do not.
        </p>

        <h2 id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. How to Know It Is Ready
        </h2>

        <p>
          The timelines get you into the right month. Two tests tell you the right day.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Sprinkle Test</h3>
        <p>
          Flick a handful of water onto a deck board in the sun and another in the shade, and watch. If it <strong>beads up and sits there</strong> in round droplets for several minutes, the wood is not ready — either it is still too wet to absorb, or there is mill glaze or water repellent on the surface. If it <strong>darkens the wood and soaks in within a minute or two</strong>, the board is open and ready to take stain. Test several boards, because a deck is never uniform: the boards near the house or under the furniture will lag the ones in full sun by weeks.
        </p>

        <p>
          The sprinkle test is not sophisticated and it does not need to be. It tests the exact thing the stain needs — can a liquid get into this wood — and it costs nothing. We do it on every deck before opening a can, new or old, and it has saved more than one job.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Moisture Meter</h3>
        <p>
          A pin-type moisture meter from General, Klein or Wagner costs somewhere between 25 and 60 dollars and gives you a number. Push the pins into the face of a board, then into the end grain, then repeat on a dozen boards around the deck. You are looking for readings <strong>below 15 percent</strong> everywhere, with the end grain — which holds water longest — not far behind the faces. If the faces read 14 and the ends read 24, the board is still drying from the inside and a stain applied now will blotch where that moisture migrates out.
        </p>

        <p>
          Cheap pin meters are calibrated for softwoods like pine and are close enough for cedar. On dense hardwoods the readings run high and should be taken as relative rather than exact. Do not worry about laboratory precision; you are looking for the difference between a board at 30 percent and a board at 14, and any meter will show that.
        </p>

        <p>
          Use both tests. A meter reading of 13 percent on a cedar board that still beads water tells you the moisture is fine and the glaze is the problem, which points you at the cleaner rather than the calendar. A treated board that soaks up water but still reads 22 percent tells you the surface has opened but the core has not, so wait another two weeks and test again.
        </p>

        <h2 id="myth" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. The "Wait a Year" Myth
        </h2>

        <p>
          You will hear it from neighbours, from some contractors and from the counter at the lumber yard: leave a new deck for a year before you stain it. It is advice from a different era of pressure-treated lumber and a different era of stain, and following it today does active harm.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here is what a year of bare exposure does to a new deck. In the first six to eight weeks, UV light breaks down the lignin in the top fibres and the surface turns grey — that grey is a layer of dead, loose wood cells no longer bonded to the board. Repeated wetting and drying raises the grain and opens surface checks, the fine lengthwise cracks that trap water and dirt. Fastener heads start to leave rust stains. Mildew moves into the shaded areas and under the furniture. On pressure-treated pine, boards that dried unevenly cup and twist. By the twelve-month mark the deck is not "seasoned"; it is a restoration job.
        </p>

        <p>
          A deck that has been left a year needs a full percarbonate clean with a stiff brush, an oxalic acid brighten, and very often a pass with a floor sander to take off the raised grain and the shallow checking before any stain will look even. That is two or three extra days of work and a couple of hundred dollars in rental and consumables, to arrive at a surface that is worse than the one you had at eight weeks.
        </p>

        <p>
          The correct version of the advice is this: wait until the wood is dry enough and the surface is open enough, which for most decks is one to three months, and then stain promptly. Do not wait a single week longer than the tests tell you to. Early protection preserves the surface; late protection has to be applied to a surface that is already damaged.
        </p>

        <h2 id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Cleaning and Brightening a New Deck
        </h2>

        <p>
          "It's brand new, it's clean" is the most expensive sentence in deck finishing. A new deck is not clean in any sense that matters to a stain. It has mill glaze from the planer. It has dirt, footprints and sawdust from construction. It has handling marks and forklift grime from the yard. Pressure-treated boards frequently have a surface residue from the treatment process, and cedar carries water-soluble extractives right at the surface. All of that has to go before the first coat, and the process is the same one we use on an old deck, just faster. The full sequence is in <a href="/how-to-prep-a-deck-for-staining/">how to prep a deck for staining</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step One: Percarbonate Cleaner</h3>
        <p>
          A sodium percarbonate deck cleaner — the oxygenated type sold by Restore-A-Deck, DEFY, Olympic and others, or the cleaner half of a two-part system — is mixed with warm water, applied to a wetted deck with a pump sprayer, and given 10 to 15 minutes to work. Scrub with a stiff-bristled deck brush on a pole, working with the grain. The cleaner lifts dirt, breaks down the glaze and mildew, and slightly opens the surface fibres. Rinse thoroughly with a garden hose or a pressure washer on a wide fan tip at no more than about 1,200 psi, keeping the wand moving and well off the wood. New wood is soft, and a pressure washer held too close will fur the surface in seconds. Avoid chlorine bleach; it whitens the surface without cleaning it and damages the lignin. Our ranked picks are in <a href="/best-deck-cleaner/">best deck cleaner</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step Two: Oxalic Acid Brightener</h3>
        <p>
          The percarbonate cleaner is alkaline and leaves the wood slightly darkened and with the pH shifted. An oxalic acid wood brightener, sprayed onto the still-damp deck and left for 10 to 20 minutes before a rinse, neutralises it, lightens tannin and rust marks, and lays the grain back down. On pressure-treated pine the difference is noticeable; on cedar and redwood it is dramatic, restoring the warm colour that makes those species worth the money. The brightener also leaves the wood at the right pH for the stain to cure evenly. It is a fifteen-minute step and it is not optional.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step Three: Dry Again, Test Again</h3>
        <p>
          You have just put a great deal of water back into the wood. Give the deck at least 48 hours of dry weather, longer if it is humid or shaded, and then repeat the sprinkle test and the meter check. Only when it passes both do you open the stain.
        </p>

        <p>
          A note on sanding: new decks almost never need it. If the boards have been furred by an over-eager pressure washer, or a few raised splinters have appeared after the wash, a light hand-sanding with 80-grit on those spots is plenty. Do not sand a whole new deck smooth; a surface finer than 80-grit closes the grain and reduces how much stain the wood will take.
        </p>

        <h2 id="first-coat" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Choosing and Applying the First Coat
        </h2>

        <p>
          The first finish a deck receives sets the pattern for everything that follows, and the choice we make on a new deck is almost always the same: a thin, penetrating oil-based stain, applied once.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Why penetrating oil.</strong> Products like Ready Seal, Cabot Australian Timber Oil, TWP and Armstrong-Clark soak into new wood rather than sitting on it. They cannot peel because they form no film. They fade gracefully and take a maintenance coat without stripping. On a new deck that will need recoating in two to three years anyway, that maintenance-friendliness is worth more than any other property. The rankings are in <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">best deck stain reviews</a>, and the pressure-treated specifics are in <a href="/best-deck-stain-for-pressure-treated-wood/">best deck stain for pressure-treated wood</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Why thin, and why one coat.</strong> New wood, once it has dried and been cleaned, is thirsty but not bottomless. A single coat, back-brushed so it is worked into the grain, is usually all a new deck will absorb. Any stain still sitting wet on the surface after 20 to 30 minutes is excess and needs to be brushed out or wiped off, or it will cure as a shiny skin that peels the next year. Most penetrating products allow a second coat only wet-on-wet, within a short window, on wood that visibly drank the first. If in doubt, stop at one; you can always add a maintenance coat next season, and you cannot easily remove an over-application. Our guide to <a href="/how-many-coats-of-deck-stain/">how many coats of deck stain</a> goes into the label-by-label detail.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Semi-transparent, not solid.</strong> New wood is the one time in a deck's life when it looks its best. A solid stain hides it behind an opaque film that will need to be maintained as paint from now on. A clear sealer, at the other extreme, offers almost no UV protection and the wood greys underneath it within a season. A semi-transparent or transparent-tinted penetrating stain protects the wood and shows it off. The comparison is in <a href="/solid-vs-semi-transparent-deck-stain/">solid vs semi-transparent deck stain</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Application.</strong> Stain boards end to end to avoid lap marks, work in the shade or on an overcast day with the surface between 50 and 90 degrees, and back-brush everything. Coat the end grain generously; it drinks far more than the face and it is where water gets in first. Do the rails and verticals with a brush or a stain pad, and keep the sprayer for the floor if you use one at all. The full method is in <a href="/how-to-stain-a-deck/">how to stain a deck</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          7. Seasonal Timing and Missed Windows
        </h2>

        <p>
          Because the wait is driven by drying, the season the deck was built in changes everything.
        </p>

        <p>
          A deck built in <strong>April or May</strong> from wet pressure-treated lumber in most of the US will be ready in July or August — right in the heart of the staining season, with warm boards, long dry spells and time for the finish to cure before autumn. This is the ideal case.
        </p>

        <p>
          A deck built in <strong>late summer</strong> may just make it before the weather turns. Test aggressively from six weeks onward, and if it passes in September or early October with a run of dry days above 50 degrees forecast, stain it. If not, do not force it. A deck stained onto cold, damp wood in November will peel in spring.
        </p>

        <p>
          A deck built in <strong>autumn or winter</strong> will not dry meaningfully until the following spring. Leave it, sweep off the leaves and debris so mildew does not get a foothold, and plan to clean, brighten and stain in May or June. It will grey somewhat over the winter; the percarbonate clean takes that off. We go into the month-by-month detail in <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-stain-a-deck/">best time of year to stain a deck</a> and into the conditions on the day in <a href="/what-temperature-to-stain-a-deck/">what temperature to stain a deck</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If You Missed the Window and It Went Grey</h3>
        <p>
          It happens. Life gets in the way, the deck sits for a season or two, and now it is silver-grey with black mildew spots under the planters. This is recoverable, and it is exactly what the cleaning products are designed for. The grey is a surface layer of UV-degraded fibre a few thousandths of an inch deep; a percarbonate cleaner and a stiff brush lift most of it, and the oxalic brightener brings back much of the original colour. Let it dry for 48 to 72 hours and test.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the boards have raised grain that catches your socks, or the surface has checked enough that the cleaner cannot reach the bottom of the cracks, rent a floor sander and take the deck back with 60-grit, then 80-grit. Then clean and brighten again to remove the sanding dust and open the grain, dry, test and stain. It is a weekend more than it would have been at eight weeks, but you end up with a surface that will take a finish perfectly well. What you should not do is stain over the grey. The stain bonds to loose fibre, the fibre releases, and you have a peeling deck with a fraction of the expected life.
        </p>

        <p>
          The one honest caveat: if a pressure-treated deck has been left bare for several years and the boards are cupped, deeply checked and splintering, no amount of prep returns it to new. At that point the choice is between a solid stain or resurfacer that fills the damage and a board-by-board replacement of the worst offenders, and it is worth having someone look at the fasteners and joists before spending money on either.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Staining wet pressure-treated wood the week it was built</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Boards at 40 percent moisture content cannot absorb stain. It cures on the surface as a film and lifts within a year as the wood shrinks underneath it. Test first, every time.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Waiting a full year because somebody said so</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Twelve months of bare exposure greys, checks and raises the grain. You end up sanding and restoring a deck that would have taken stain perfectly at two months.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Skipping the clean because the deck is new</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Mill glaze, yard dirt and treatment residue all stop stain penetrating. A percarbonate clean and an oxalic brighten take an afternoon and double the life of the first coat.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Two heavy coats on new wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">New wood takes one thin coat. The second one has nowhere to go and cures as a glossy skin that peels in translucent flakes. Save it for the maintenance coat in two years.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Judging by the calendar instead of the wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A deck in Phoenix and a deck in Portland built the same day are months apart in readiness. The sprinkle test and a 30-dollar meter are the only calendar that counts.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should you wait to stain a new pressure-treated deck?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Standard wet pressure-treated lumber needs at least four weeks of warm, dry weather and commonly two to three months before it drops below 15 percent moisture content. In a humid or cool climate it can take longer. Confirm with a moisture meter or the sprinkle test rather than the calendar. Kiln-dried-after-treatment (KDAT) lumber can be cleaned and stained within a week or two.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain a new deck right away?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Only if it was built from KDAT pressure-treated lumber or kiln-dried cedar, and only after a clean and brighten to remove mill glaze. Ordinary pressure-treated boards from the lumber yard arrive saturated and will not accept stain for weeks.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to wait a year to stain a new deck?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">No, and you should not. A year of bare exposure greys the surface, opens checks in the boards, raises the grain and photo-degrades the top fibres. Most new decks are ready in one to three months. Waiting a year means a full clean, brighten and often a sanding before the first coat.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my new deck is dry enough to stain?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Sprinkle water on several boards. If it beads and sits, wait. If it darkens the wood and soaks in within a minute or two, the wood is ready. A pin moisture meter reading below 15 percent on the board faces and ends confirms it; most stain labels specify somewhere between 12 and 19 percent.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I clean a brand-new deck before staining?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes. New wood carries mill glaze, dirt from the yard and handling marks, and pressure-treated boards often have surface residue from the treatment. A percarbonate deck cleaner followed by an oxalic acid brightener opens the grain and gives the stain something to soak into.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long to wait to stain a new cedar deck?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Two to eight weeks in most cases. Cedar and redwood are usually kiln-dried at the mill to around 15 to 19 percent, so moisture is rarely the problem. Mill glaze is. Clean and brighten the deck, let it dry for two or three days, and stain it before the surface starts to grey.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain composite decking?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Capped composite such as current Trex, TimberTech and Fiberon, and all PVC decking, does not get stained. The cap is a plastic shell and stain has nothing to penetrate. Older uncapped composite can be cleaned and, if faded, coated with a composite-specific product, but ordinary wood stain will fail on it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What if I waited too long and the deck went grey?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Grey is dead surface fibre and it comes off. Clean with a percarbonate cleaner and a stiff brush, brighten with oxalic acid, and let it dry. If the boards have raised grain or shallow checking, a pass with 60- to 80-grit paper on a floor sander restores a stainable surface. Then finish as you would a new deck.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

